<section xml:id="section-activity-slo1">
  <title>Activity for SLO 1</title>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-slo1-review">
    <title>Conceptual Review</title>
    <p>
      You can test your learning from the video (and your own
      background knowledge) with the following review questions.
      Conceptual questions on quizes may be taken from these review
      questions. 
    </p>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why do we want formal proofs of the derivative rules?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          In mathematics, the standard of truth is proof. It doesn't
          matter that a rule <sq>seems to work</sq>. Mathematical
          ideas aren't considered fact until they have a proof. The
          derivative rules work and you could just accept them on that
          practical basis. But, mathematically, there would also be a
          worry that there is some situation, some special case, where
          the rule doesn't apply. A proof gets rid of that worry: it
          is a conclusive argument that, in the logic of mathematics,
          the rules will absolutely always work. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-activity-derivative-proofs">
    <title>Calculation Questions: Derivative Proofs</title>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Prove that the derivative of cosine is negative sine. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          I follow the same steps as the proof for sine. I start with
          the limit definition of the derivative. 
          <md>
            \frac{d}{dx} \cos x = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{\cos (x
            + h) - \cos x}{h}
          </md>
          Again, I use the trig identity for the cosine of a sum.  
          <md>
            = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{ \cos x \cos h - \sin x \sin h
            - \cos x}{h} 
          </md>
          Then I can split up this limit and group it into two. I
          split it so that all the cosine pieces are together. 
          <md>
            = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{ \cos x \cos h - \cos x}{h} 
            + \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{ \sin x \sin h }{h} 
          </md>
          Then I factor and pull anything out of the limit that
          doesn't involve the limit variable <m>h</m>. 
          <md>
            = \cos x \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{ \cos h - 1}{h} 
            + \sin x \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{ \sin h }{h} 
          </md>
          Then I apply the known limits for ratios with trig
          functions. 
          <md>
            = \cos x (0) - \sin x (1) = - \sin x
          </md>
          This completes the argument. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Prove the other two parts of linearity that weren't proved
          in the notes. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          I follow very similar steps to the proof for the first part
          in the notes. Here is the limit definition applied to the
          difference of two functions. 
          <md>
            \frac{d}{dx} (f(x) - g(x)) \lim_{h \rightarrow 0}
            \frac{f(x + h) - g(x + h) - (f(x) - g(x))}{h}
          </md>
          I just group this number into the two appropriate piece and
          split up the fraction and the limit. I am careful to note
          that when I distribute the negative, two negative will just
          be a positive. 
          <md>
            = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{f(x + h) - f(x)}{h}
            - \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{g(x + h) - g(x)}{h} =
            \frac{df}{dx} - \frac{dg}{dx} 
          </md>
          That completes the proof for a difference. Now I use the
          limit definition for a function multiplied by a constant. 
          <md>
            \frac{d}{dx} (cf(x)) = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{cf(x +
            h) - (cf(x))}{h} 
          </md>
          I can factor the <m>c</m> out of the numerator. 
          <md>
            \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{c(f(x + h) - f(x))}{h}
          </md>
          Then, according to the limit laws, I can factor the <m>c</m>
          out of the limt
          <md>
            c \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{(f(x + h) - f(x))}{h} = c
            \frac{df}{dx}
          </md>
          I'm let with the usual imit definition, so this completes
          the proof. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
</section>
